cyclopedia/thumbs/louis-delgres.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Delgrès</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Delgrès was a free man of colour from Martinique and an officer of the French Revolutionary army who led resistance in Guadeloupe to the restoration of slavery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-edouard-bouet-willaumez"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-edouard-bouet-willaumez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez was a French naval officer and colonial administrator who explored and charted the West African coast in the 1830s and 1840s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-fadrique"><span class="enc-name">Louis Fadrique</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Fadrique was count of Salona in central Greece and a member of the Fadrique family, a branch of the royal house of Sicily.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-faidherbe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Faidherbe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Faidherbe was a French general and colonial governor of Senegal from 1854 to 1861 and 1863 to 1865.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-of-etruria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I of Etruria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I was the first king of Etruria, a son of Duke Ferdinand of Parma.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I of Hungary</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I of Hungary, known as Louis the Great, was king of Hungary and Croatia from 1342 and king of Poland from 1370 until his death in 1382.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-count-of-flanders"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Count of Flanders</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, known as Louis of Nevers, was count of Flanders, Nevers and Rethel from 1322 until his death at the Battle of Crécy in 1346.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-bavaria"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, called the Kelheimer, was Duke of Bavaria of the house of Wittelsbach from 1183 to 1231.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-duke-of-orleans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, Duke of Orléans, was the younger brother of King Charles VI of France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-grand-duke-of-hesse"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-grand-duke-of-hesse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Grand Duke of Hesse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I was the last landgrave of Hesse-Darmstadt, as Louis X from 1790, and the first grand duke of Hesse.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen from 1603 until his death, was the first ruler of the separate principality of Anhalt-Köthen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II de La Trémoille</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II de La Trémoille was a French commander who served Charles VIII, Louis XII and Francis I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Hungary</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Hungary was the Jagiellonian king of Hungary and Bohemia from 1516.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Italy was the eldest son of Lothair I, king of Italy from 844 and Carolingian emperor from 855.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-poitiers"><span class="enc-name">Louis II of Poitiers</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II of Poitiers was the last count of Valentinois and Diois in the Rhône valley, of the Poitiers family that had ruled there since the 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Count of Flanders</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, known as Louis of Male, was count of Flanders from 1346 to 1384 and also count of Nevers and Rethel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, Duke of Bavaria, known as the Strict, was a Wittelsbach duke and Count Palatine of the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon, called the Good Duke, was a French prince who fought in the Hundred Years' War and led a crusade to Mahdia in 1390.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-iii-grand-duke-of-hesse"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-iii-grand-duke-of-hesse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis III, Grand Duke of Hesse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis III was grand duke of Hesse from 1848 to 1877, succeeding his father Louis II during the revolution.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IV of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IV, called d'Outremer (&quot;from overseas&quot;), was King of West Francia from 936 to 954.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IV, called the Bavarian, was a Wittelsbach duke elected King of Germany in 1314 and crowned emperor in Rome in 1328 without papal consent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-marburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-marburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Marburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IV was Landgrave of Hesse-Marburg from 1567 to 1604, the second son of Philip the Magnanimous, who received about a quarter of Hesse, centred on Marburg and its…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-thuringia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-iv-landgrave-of-thuringia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IV, Landgrave of Thuringia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IV was Landgrave of Thuringia from 1217 to 1227, a son of Hermann I, patron of the Wartburg poets.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ix-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IX of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IX of France, king from 1226 to 1270, was revered as a model Christian ruler and canonised in 1297.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis IX, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis IX, Duke of Bavaria-Landshut, called the Rich, ruled from 1450 to 1479.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-jean-jacques-acaau"><span class="enc-name">Louis Jean-Jacques Acaau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Jean-Jacques Acaau (died 1846) was a Haitian peasant leader and former army officer who led the piquets, an uprising of black smallholders in southern Haiti, in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-jolliet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-jolliet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Jolliet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Jolliet was a French-Canadian explorer and fur trader born in Quebec.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-mountbatten.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Mountbatten</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Mountbatten was a British naval officer who became the last Viceroy of India in 1947.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-burgundy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis of Burgundy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis of Burgundy was a younger son of Duke Robert II of Burgundy who married Matilda of Hainaut and became prince of Achaea in 1313.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-mousson"><span class="enc-name">Louis of Mousson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis of Mousson was an 11th-century nobleman who held the county of Montbéliard and, through his marriage to Sophie of Bar around 1038, the county of Bar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-pasteur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Pasteur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Pasteur was a nineteenth-century French scientist whose work on microbes, fermentation and vaccines founded modern microbiology.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-philippe-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Philippe I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Philippe I was King of the French from 1830 to 1848.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-riel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Riel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Riel was a Métis leader of the Red River settlement.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-rudolph-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-rudolph-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Rudolph, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis Rudolph was a younger son of Duke Anthony Ulrich of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, who gave him Blankenburg in the Harz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-rwagasore.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis Rwagasore</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prince Louis Rwagasore was a Burundian nationalist, son of Mwami Mwambutsa IV, who led the UPRONA party to victory in the elections of September 1961.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-blind.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the Blind</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the Blind, son of Boso, was king of Provence, or Lower Burgundy, from 887 and king of Italy from 900.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-child.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the Child</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the Child was king of East Francia from 900 to 911, the son of Emperor Arnulf of Carinthia and the last of the East Frankish Carolingians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-german.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the German</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the German was a son of Louis the Pious and king of East Francia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-pious.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the Pious</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the Pious was the son and successor of Charlemagne, ruling the Carolingian Empire from 814 to 840.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-stammerer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the Stammerer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the Stammerer was the son of Charles the Bald, who made him king of Aquitaine in 867.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-the-younger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis the Younger</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis the Younger was king of East Francia from 876 to 882, a son of Louis the German.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-v-duke-of-bavaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-v-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis V, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis V, called Louis the Brandenburger, was Margrave of Brandenburg from 1323 to 1351 and later Duke of Upper Bavaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-v-landgrave-of-hesse-darmstadt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-v-landgrave-of-hesse-darmstadt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis V, Landgrave of Hesse-Darmstadt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis V was landgrave of Hesse-Darmstadt from 1596 to 1626.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-vi-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis VI of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis VI, called the Fat, was king of France from 1108 to 1137.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-vii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis VII of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis VII was King of the Franks from 1137 to 1180, of the Capetian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis VII, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis VII the Bearded was duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt from 1413.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-viii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis VIII of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis VIII, called the Lion, was king of France from 1223 to 1226 and son of Philip II Augustus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-duke-of-bavaria"><span class="enc-name">Louis VIII, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis VIII, known as Louis the Hunchback, was duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt and the son of Louis VII the Bearded.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis William, Margrave of Baden-Baden from 1677, was an imperial field marshal known as the Turkish Louis for his victories over the Ottomans, above all at Slankamen…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XI</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XI was King of France from 1461 to 1483.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XII of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XII, born at Blois as duke of Orléans, became king of France in 1498.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xiii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XIII of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XIII, son of Henry IV, was king of France from 1610.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XIV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XIV was King of France from 1643 to 1715, whose long reign marked the height of royal absolutism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XV was king of France from 1715 to 1774, succeeding his great-grandfather Louis XIV at the age of five.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xvi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XVI</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XVI was King of France from 1774.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xviii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Louis XVIII</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis XVIII, brother of Louis XVI, was king of France from 1814 to 1824, apart from the Hundred Days of 1815.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-cardinal-of-bar"><span class="enc-name">Louis, Cardinal of Bar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Louis of Bar was a French churchman and cardinal who inherited the Duchy of Bar in 1415, after his brothers were killed at the Battle of Agincourt.</span></a></li></ul>
